1
00:03:32,268 --> 00:03:57,243
14th regular meeting, Medford City Council, August 4th, 2026 is called to order. Mr. Clerk, please call the roll. Councilor Kelly. Present. Councilor Lemming. Present. Councilor Malate. Present. Councilor Scalpelli. Present. Councilor Singh. Present. Vice President Lazaro. Present. And President Pierce. Present. Seven present, none absent. The meeting is called to order. Please rise to salute the flag.

2
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:33,575
Announcements, accolades, remembrances, reports, and records. 26135 offered by Council Vice President Lazaro. Resolution in remembrance of John Westwater. Whereas, John Westwater lived on Usher Road, steps from the West Medford train station. He taught middle school at St. Joseph's in Medford for many years, whereas his affectionate nickname was Mr. Sister. His favorite hobbies were camping and going for walks. He loved nature, his home, and his neighborhood.

3
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:58,111
Whereas on July 20th, an Amtrak train was passing through West Medford train station just down the street from where John lived. He was crossing the tracks and was hit. John died from his injuries. He was 77 years old. Now, therefore, be it resolved by the Medford City Council that we share our deepest condolences with Mr. Westwater's family and loved ones and express our hope that this senseless tragedy will not be in vain and will not be repeated. Be it further resolved that we will work with the MBTA and Amtrak

4
00:04:58,735 --> 00:05:14,058
A quasi-governmental corporation created by Congress in 1970 to preserve intercity passenger rail to make sorely needed safety improvements to the West Medford commuter and Amtrak station so this will never happen again. Council Vice President Lazaro. Thank you.

5
00:05:14,868 --> 00:05:39,657
A year and a half ago, I wrote a very similar condolence resolution to this about Dan Dill, who lived very close to where I live now. And he was walking his dog across Mystic Valley Parkway. And then I wrote a very similar resolution to this

6
00:05:40,147 --> 00:06:06,505
a couple of months after that about Arthur Weber, who was crossing a street on a morning walk. And now I wrote it about John Westwater. He lived in Medford for a very long time, and he taught Merilungo Kern at St. Joe's.

7
00:06:07,653 --> 00:06:25,827
And I spoke with his sister this week, last week, and she told me that he used to volunteer every week at St. Francis House, which is a shelter in Boston, which is actually where I work now.

8
00:06:26,165 --> 00:06:46,988
She is donating his clothes to the clothing room at the shelter where I'm there all the time. John lived right at Usher Road, which had for a long time before they made some changes to the station.

9
00:06:47,545 --> 00:07:10,073
what's called a desire path from the end of Usher Road up to the train station and across the tracks. There was a hole in the fence and there was a dirt path up the sort of hill and people would just walk across the tracks. So I take the train actually every day from West Medford, that train station, to Boston to go to St. Francis House and

10
00:07:10,782 --> 00:07:39,436
When I come home, everybody who gets off the train just walks straight across the tracks, not on a concrete path, not at the crossing where the cars stop. And I did it today. You walk on the train tracks. Because West Medford has an at-grade train station. And when John Westwater was crossing, he was going to get, he was going to Magnificent Muffin, according to his sister.

11
00:07:39,976 --> 00:08:03,652
And Amtrak trains come through. They don't stop at the station. So they're still investigating exactly what happened, but he was hit by the Amtrak train. He died from his injuries. And John was in the early stages of dementia and was a very vulnerable person because of that.

12
00:08:04,192 --> 00:08:32,272
There are a lot of vulnerable people in that neighborhood, older people and children and people with disabilities. And I believe it is the obligation of people in government to make sure that infrastructure exists to prevent vulnerable people from experiencing an accident that is avoidable.

13
00:08:33,082 --> 00:08:47,662
So even if he crossed the train tracks where he shouldn't have, that should not have been easy. And we should make it our business to prevent that from happening.

14
00:08:49,012 --> 00:09:12,755
So I've been in conversation with our state rep who represents that part of Medford, Sean Garbally, and he told me he was speaking with the MBTA about having a meeting, and they offered him a date to have a meeting that was months away. And he said that wasn't acceptable, and he still hasn't, they haven't gotten back to him, as far as I know, about a sooner date.

15
00:09:13,413 --> 00:09:35,502
This will now be the only thing that I think about until we have a meeting and we make the changes that we need to make to make this train station safe for everybody who lives in Medford and everybody who passes through Medford and everybody who needs to cross the street and everybody who wants to cross the street slightly earlier, maybe they're running late. I'm running late.

16
00:09:36,228 --> 00:10:00,157
all day, every day, all the time. So if there's any shortcut I could take, I will take it. And please prevent me from getting hit by a train and dying. This is what we're here to do. Something as simple as a fence between the two train tracks, a fence that doesn't have any holes in it on the sides would be baseline. But I think what we should really have is

17
00:10:00,646 --> 00:10:29,232
pedestrian bridge and it will take a long time and it will be really expensive, but somebody died. And that's horrible and this was just a matter of time. So it appalls me that this happened to anybody, but especially somebody who was engaging in an activity that he loved.

18
00:10:30,785 --> 00:10:59,033
His sister Meg said specifically he loved his neighborhood. He loved his home. He loved walking. He loved going to the muffin shop. Like these are not things that should be dangerous. And evidently it took a long time to convince the MBTA to do the ADA upgrades that they did. It took, I know it took a very long time to have them fix the ADA upgrade so it wasn't disturbing the neighborhood. All of this,

19
00:11:00,653 --> 00:11:30,505
shouldn't be this hard, but I know that we will work as hard as we need to to make sure that we keep our residents safe. But we failed in this case. And if we can loop in Amtrak, I don't think that they are absolved of wrongdoing here either. If they use the tracks, they should be responsible. And I'm very sorry that somebody

20
00:11:30,843 --> 00:11:51,970
who seems like a very good person, but really anybody. This shouldn't happen to anybody. But I'm very sorry for John Westwater. I'm very sorry for his sister. I'm very sorry for all his friends and family. And I'm just sorry to have to do this again. Thank you, Vice President Lazaro. Any further comments by members of the council?

21
00:11:54,907 --> 00:12:23,746
Councillor Sang. I'll be extra brief. Just ditto to everything that's been said. It's incumbent upon everyone from the MBTA to the Amtrak to our city government and the state government to make sure that our streets and neighborhoods are safe. It's a tragedy that's happened in our city and we can and we should and we will demand better.

22
00:12:26,210 --> 00:12:54,695
Thank you, Councilor Sang. Seeing no other comments from members of the Council, I'll just say thank you for putting this resolution on. And I also think, in general, that the investment by the MBTA in the West Medford station has been sorely under-invested. We see what the Winchester Center station looks like. We see what other stations look like. And it's a safety issue. It's an economic development issue.

23
00:12:55,758 --> 00:13:25,053
you know, a fairness issue in all of these ways. And more important than anything, it's a safety issue. So I don't think that the MBTA should be making piecemeal improvements, maybe some for safety in the short term, but this is a station and an area that has been long overdue for a significant improvement. And it's going to keep people safer and be of benefit to the neighborhood. So I really hope that we can come up with a longer term plan and vision as well.

24
00:13:26,251 --> 00:13:53,217
Vice President Lazaro, and then we do have one person in public. Thank you. Sorry, I did forget to mention that I found a report from the State Auditor's Office about the most dangerous at-grade crossings on the commuter line. And West Medford is cited as one of the most highly dangerous at-grade crossings. And this was a report from 2023.

25
00:13:53,217 --> 00:14:18,479
It was given to the MBTA. There was a plan to improve safety at that time, 2023, and nothing has been done. So my, you'll be hearing more from me in the form of something that is not condolences, but just looking for the MBTA to implement its own safety recommendations.

26
00:14:21,635 --> 00:14:50,288
Thank you. I will go to public participation for anyone who wants to speak on this resolution. I will go to Eunice Brown on Zoom. Eunice, name and neighborhood for the record, you have three minutes. Hi, can you hear me? Yes. Hi, thanks very much. I just want to talk a little bit about Mr. Westwater as a teacher. I went to St. Joe's and I had Mr. Westwater for science

27
00:14:50,795 --> 00:14:54,338
in the late 70s for seventh and eighth grade.

28
00:14:55,132 --> 00:15:21,305
And apparently, by the time I had him, he'd been at St. Joe's maybe a couple of years. And I found out just recently that he was a fairly new teacher at that time, probably in his second, third year of teaching. So he was probably about 15 years older than a bunch of 13-year-olds.

29
00:15:21,980 --> 00:15:44,035
He was a terrific teacher. He was well loved by the students. As I said, he was a science teacher and he lit the fire for science into an awful lot of his legion of students that he had over the years. If you go over to the Medford Dearly Departed site, there are close to 100 comments.

30
00:15:44,238 --> 00:16:09,989
on his obituary and most of them expressed just that, that they love science in large part because of him. He also started the St. Joe's boys basketball team and a lot of the guys spoke very highly of him in that regard as well.

31
00:16:09,989 --> 00:16:39,166
But the thing that struck me and struck an awful lot of other people who commented he instituted an annual end of year camping trip. I believe for the 7th graders and that trip is etched in my memory and the memory of many of his other students. Of course, there was plenty of science involved in the camping trip. I think we went out to Otter River State Park in Winchendon at that time and.

32
00:16:39,655 --> 00:17:05,828
it was just, it was an incredible experience in so many regards, of course, the science, but getting out into nature, not being around our parents, of course, we had chaperones as well. And the usual, you know, junior high school, you know, shenanigans, although we were a bunch of Catholic school kids. So maybe the shenanigans were not that bad. But

33
00:17:06,419 --> 00:17:35,697
Of the 100 comments on Medford Dearly Departed and another dozen or so on the St. Joe's alumni group, you'll see that he was very well loved, very well regarded, even from students that he had 50 plus years ago, still consider him to be one of the best teachers that they ever had. I'm glad Counselor Lozaro spoke to his sister. I wonder if she speaks to her again,

34
00:17:35,697 --> 00:18:03,659
Maybe you could point. I think her name is Meg to the Medford dearly departed site or print out the comments for her and email them to her or something so that she can see just how highly regarded her brother was. Mr Westwater was one of a kind and I'm so so sad that he met such a tragic and untimely passing. So thank you very much.

35
00:18:05,920 --> 00:18:31,992
Thank you so much for your comment. Excuse me. Thank you so much for your comment and for your memories of Mr. Westwater. On the motion of Vice President Lazaro, seconded by. Seconded by Councilor Sang. Mr. Clerk, please call the roll. Councilor Kellyanne. Yes. Councilor Lemming. Yes. Councilor Malate. Yes. Councilor Scapelli. Yes. Councilor Sang. Yes. Vice President Lazaro.

36
00:18:32,836 --> 00:19:01,388
And President Pierce. Yes, 7 in the affirmative, none in the negative. The motion passes. Please rise for a moment of silence if you're able. Thank you.

37
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:28,912
Records and reports of committees. Is there a motion to join and approve the records and reports of committees by Councilor Seng, seconded by? Seconded by Councilor Malauulu. Records. The records of the meeting of June 23rd were passed to Vice President Lazaro. Vice President Lazaro, how did you find those records? Found them in order and motion to approve. Great. The records of the meeting of July 14th were passed to Councilor Lemming. Councilor Lemming, how did you find those records? I found them in order.

38
00:19:30,565 --> 00:19:56,705
Reports of committees, 19-070, offered by President Behr's Committee of the Whole, July 14th, 2026. This was a meeting on the tree ordinance, and we advanced the public tree ordinance part of that forward for final consideration after review by further minor review by DPW and legal, I believe. And Vice President Lazaro, Public Health and Community Safety Subcommittee, or Committee, sorry.

39
00:19:57,329 --> 00:20:25,915
Yes, this was a meeting where we discussed a resolution by Councilor Callahan about enforcing regulations in Medford around the Fourth Amendment and ICE officers. Great. Thank you. On the motion of Councilor Sang, seconded by Councilor Mullane, I think it was, to join and approve the records and reports of committees, Mr. Clark, please call the roll.

40
00:20:28,295 --> 00:20:52,291
Councilor Callahan? Yes. Councilor Lemming? Yes. Councilor Malayne? Yes. Councilor Scarpelli? Yes. Councilor Singh? Yes. Vice President Lozano? Yes. President Beers? Yes. I have an affirmative and a negative. The motion passes. Refer to committee for further discussion. 26138 offered by Councilor Malayne and Councilor Singh. Resolution to update the noise ordinance to be referred to the Resident Services Committee.

41
00:20:52,814 --> 00:21:04,745
Whereas the city's noise ordinance was last updated in 1974 and is lacking any recent updates to types of noise that can impact residents and whereas residents of Medford have shared a variety of ways in which different noise levels have been impacting their daily lives.

42
00:21:05,183 --> 00:21:32,842
And whereas noise levels can also be considered a health hazard and is left to the community to help regulate and advise on noise restrictions. And whereas the American Public Health Association has stated that loud noise causes hearing loss, tinnitus, and can contribute to non-auditory health problems, chronic noise can cause annoyance, sleep disruption, and stress that contribute to disease. Whereas the AAPHA policy statements below relate to this update by advocating for the following, reducing environmental stressors, rectifying the disproportionate burden

43
00:21:33,314 --> 00:21:56,112
hazards borne by disadvantaged populations and encouraging actions to mitigate adverse effects be resolved by the City Council that the Resident Services and Public Engagement Committee review the current noise ordinance to update its language, function, type and current usage. I'll recognize Councilor Malauulu, then Councilor Sang, then Councilor Scarpelli. This is referred to committee for further discussion, so please try to keep it a little brief. Councilor Malauulu.

44
00:21:56,787 --> 00:22:23,568
Thank you. Yes, you know, what was really interesting was the number of emails that I have been receiving, I'm sure others have received, about different types of noise complaints and different ways that they've been impacting residents. And especially one particular resident got very in-depth about the health hazards and the things that we should really be looking at. So after looking at our own noise ordinance, which really just lists two different types,

45
00:22:23,568 --> 00:22:41,928
operation of plant or machinery and performance of certain work and then singing and music, sound devices or machines. They just really lacked a lot of different things that are happening right now that residents have reached out and had comments and wanted to make sure that we were looking to address.

46
00:22:41,928 --> 00:23:11,864
So some of the things that we're hoping to go through and I think part of the reason why getting it moved to the committee is that we do want to have public input. We do want to have different residents reach out and be able to suggest other things that we might not be able to or may have missed as we go through this process. I personally have been researching different noise ordinances from Cambridge and Somerville and just right off the bat there was just all different types of definitions that are,

47
00:23:11,864 --> 00:23:29,060
lacking from what we currently have in Medford, not only between that, between residential and commercial and things that are measurable versus non-measurable when it comes to noise impact. So given that and given a lot of the information I was reading about the health impact felt like it was necessary to

48
00:23:29,060 --> 00:23:50,204
take a real look at this, really review some of the comments that have come in from residents, hoping that more will join us and have this conversation just to make sure that we're addressing kind of what some of the bigger complaints are people are seeing and be able to get something put in place that is also working with the city in terms of being enforceable as well.

49
00:23:50,204 --> 00:23:59,958
So, more to come, we just want to start having the conversation, get it out to people to recognize that we want to start working on this and really welcome any other types of collaboration as well.

50
00:24:02,708 --> 00:24:24,275
Great. Councillor Sang. Our current ordinance is just something that is out of date. It's something that's really difficult to enforce. A main mission of many of us here on the city council is to modernize our code of ordinances to bring into the 21st century and to be something that we can actually enforce to improve the quality of life for our residents.

51
00:24:26,941 --> 00:24:34,484
Thank you for my colleagues to bring this forward. I think it's very important.

52
00:24:34,940 --> 00:25:04,927
across the street from the RuHall plotted this, so I just wanted to share that as I received some emails. And again, it's a great business, but at the same time, we're still having the same concerns. So my addition to this would be if we can, as part of this or as a second meeting, talking with the building, the code enforcement officers, what we're seeing right now is in all of our ordinances where there's some sort of penalty, we are really not holding

53
00:25:04,927 --> 00:25:26,392
our citizens to task when it comes to fees and fines, because we don't have anything with teeth. So as much as we're putting into demanding or pushing our code enforcement officers to follow through with fees and fines, whether it's snow removal, it's different than snow removal. I talked to Council Beers about that.

54
00:25:26,628 --> 00:25:47,924
with the noise ordinance, with other ordinances. We had a resident call, just to share some information, that they had an illegal business in a residential area. They went out, code enforcement fined them a few times. From what I gather on Friday, the resident

55
00:25:48,447 --> 00:26:15,818
was so upset that the neighbors called that he decided to blow his gutters out with leaf blowers and had his crew set up ladders along the building. So a residential neighbor with a family really had an issue. So code enforcement shared an interesting fact that I think that we should take upon at this meeting, if my counselors allow, that general law chapter 40U really

56
00:26:17,455 --> 00:26:45,046
adds to our ordinances and gives it teeth where any of the fines and fees that aren't being paid or held, they're not held accountable to pay, then it is then pushed onto the lien process. So then they do have to be held accountable. So this is something we should talk with the city treasurer and our code enforcement officer and our legal team, if we have one, to try to figure out what we can do to put teeth to what we've been asking for

57
00:26:45,046 --> 00:27:14,510
for many of our audiences. So I'm already too winded. I know Councilor Baez is burning a hole through his screen, but if my colleagues would permit that we try to make that as an addition to the noise audience. So not only will we move forward to some solid movement, but at the same time also add the teeth that's needed when we do want to follow through with it. So thank you, Mr. President. Thank you, Councilor Scarpelli.

58
00:27:14,932 --> 00:27:43,788
On the refer to committee, we don't do public participation, but there will be a committee meeting for public participation. Any further discussion by members of the council? Seeing none, on the motion of Councilor Millane to refer this paper to the Resident Services and Public Engagement Committee seconded by Councilor Sang, the proponents of this. Mr. Clerk, please call the roll. Councilor Kelly? Yes. Councilor Lemming? Yes. Councilor Millane?

59
00:27:44,952 --> 00:27:57,389
Councilor Scapelli? Yes. Councilor Singh? Yes. Vice President Lazaro? Yes. And President Pierce? Yes. I have the affirmative, none in the negative. The motion passes and the paper is referred. Councilor Callahan?

