Item 26136, resolution to demand Tufts reinstate graduate worker facing retaliation and retribution.

107
00:47:30,927 --> 00:48:00,661
offered by President Behrs, whereas a Tufts University international graduate worker recently faced retaliation by her advisor for reporting that they required her to work a significant amount of hours as an unpaid teaching assistant, which violated her union contract. Later, the graduate worker was removed from her doctoral program and, whereas the worker and her union identified that there were multiple contract violations over the course of the year and the worker has experienced retaliation for protected union activity about her working conditions,

108
00:48:01,066 --> 00:48:15,342
And whereas, after experiencing this retaliation, Tufts University informed the worker on June 24th that she was disenrolled from her doctoral program and transferred to the Masters of Science program, forcing her out of the university in August.

109
00:48:15,950 --> 00:48:40,874
Whereas, after being forced out of the doctoral program that she intended to complete, her student visa is expected to expire in September 2026. This worker was not even given the opportunity to find a different advisor at Tufts and she is not being given sufficient time to apply to other doctoral programs or conduct a proper job search. Now, therefore, be it resolved by the Medford City Council that we call on Tufts to immediately re-enroll the student in her doctoral program.

110
00:48:41,785 --> 00:49:07,823
Be it further resolved that we call on Tufts to award this worker compensation for losing her summer stipend and losing her doctoral program enrollment after reporting bargaining contract violations, provide transitional support to move to a new lab to remain in the program, and cease any future tolerance of retribution for protected union activity, especially at a university that claims to be committed to advancing equity and justice. President Paris.

111
00:49:12,312 --> 00:49:32,377
Thank you, Vice President Lazaro, and I want to thank SEIU Local 509 and the Tufts Graduate Workers Union for their advocacy for their comrade and their fellow graduate worker at Tufts. There are, you know, a number of

112
00:49:32,579 --> 00:49:47,868
really egregious violations of this person's rights that have been brought up, and most importantly, that there is, you know, a threat to this person's legal status here in the country because of the actions of the, you know,

113
00:49:47,868 --> 00:50:05,215
management at Tufts. I have been in communication with folks from the grad workers union at Tufts for about a month about this now. I know that because of the sensitivity of this, this is really someone's colleagues and

114
00:50:05,215 --> 00:50:32,435
friends standing up for them when they can't be public about this themselves. And I think that really speaks to the importance of labor unions and also just the power of community and the community at Tufts. I know a number of people who are colleagues with the person who's being impacted by these contract violations and being put in danger by the actions of the Tufts administration. And I'm just really

115
00:50:32,435 --> 00:50:49,630
Frustrated you know there are so many instances where we see Tufts not being a good neighbor and not showing up for our community the way we need them to. And I think it's important for us to work to hold Tufts accountable on all fronts not just when they don't pay us enough in pilot.

116
00:50:49,630 --> 00:51:10,032
not just when they, you know, aren't showing up the way we need in terms of their institutional planning and development, but also when they're not treating their workers fairly or, you know, or their students fairly. And we've shown up on all of those fronts as a city council over the last five years. It's important for us to join together with

117
00:51:10,032 --> 00:51:40,019
with the folks on campus to advocate for justice and fairness and for Tufts to be a good neighbor, and this is part of that. So I think this is squarely within our charge as a city council, and really what we need more of from the city of Medford is bringing folks together who are pushing Tufts management to do the right thing, be a good neighbor, treat people fairly, because they're a huge institution with a budget much bigger than the city of Medford, with an endowment much bigger than the city of Medford,

118
00:51:40,019 --> 00:52:04,167
with state laws and loopholes that give them much more power than us as well. And we're only going to be able to effectively push back if we join together and do that together. So I really want to see Tufts do the right thing here, just like I want to see them do the right thing with all of the other actions that they take here in our city. And I did receive an email response from Tufts that I will read.

119
00:52:04,927 --> 00:52:24,890
Now, President Behrs, I notice that resolution 26136 is on tonight's city council agenda relating to Tufts University. Below, please find a statement from the university. I respectfully ask the statement be added to the public record and read at tonight's council meeting. Statement follows. Graduate student workers are an important part of our workforce. We appreciate their contribution to our community.

120
00:52:24,890 --> 00:52:48,903
The university respects our union agreements and the processes to address workplace concerns. We also take great care to respect our students' and employees' privacy, consistent with best practices based on the applicable state and federal laws, such as the Federal Educational Rights and Privacy Act, FERPA. In fact, because student educational records, including information about students' academic progress, standing fall squarely within FERPA protections, we will not comment on this matter specifically.

121
00:52:48,903 --> 00:53:08,967
However, please know we engage directly with SEIU Local 509 on matters that are raised through the applicable grievance processes, and you will not be surprised to know that we do not always agree with the union's allegations in certain matters. This said, the grievance processes available to our employees were negotiated with the union and offer a robust, fair, and transparent process which facilitates resolution of the matters for the parties involved.

122
00:53:08,967 --> 00:53:24,509
We've asked Ahsan Ali, our director of labor relations, to check in with the union about the concerns you have raised to ensure that the university has addressed these issues through the appropriate process. Sincerely, Rocco DiRico, associate vice president of government and community relations, Tufts University.

123
00:53:25,522 --> 00:53:55,458
And I appreciate that statement and I do know that we have some folks here tonight, colleagues of the person who is facing this unjust treatment who might want to speak. But I really, you know, encourage the Director of Labor Relations to check in with the union because when I went to the rally that they held and when I checked in with them tonight, I do not think that the university has addressed these issues through the appropriate process as the email has been quoted. So I hope the university will do that now. Thank you.

124
00:53:56,318 --> 00:54:25,445
Thank you, President Behrs. I will go to councilor comments before public comment, and then we will hear from everybody. Thank you so much. Councilor Scarpelli. Thank you. Thank you, Council Behrs, for bringing this forward. This is a serious situation, and I did contact Mr. DiRico, and he said he wanted to make sure he reiterated that Tufts is listening to this council, and that he asked Mr. Ali to

125
00:54:25,715 --> 00:54:40,700
reach out tomorrow to the president's office because they are truly trying to work through a resolution. So whether it was said, but now it's it's out there. I think that we have a platform that hopefully that

126
00:54:40,987 --> 00:55:10,450
you have an audience tomorrow and that we could support this member in making sure that they're safe and unharmed. So to me, that's a good sign. I appreciate the council to bring this forward. I think that we're seeing that when we do get involved, people are listening and Mr. DiRico never turns away. He did listen. We did talk and he did give some guidance for Mr. Ali to reach out and let's see what happens. So again, we meet again in September.

127
00:55:10,450 --> 00:55:25,756
You know, I welcome the interaction and hopefully we get to a resolution to protect a valuable member of the Tufts community. So thank you. Thank you. I'm going to go to Councilor Lemming on Zoom.

128
00:55:27,325 --> 00:55:51,558
Yes, thank you. And thank you so much to SEIU for bringing this forward. I'd just like to say that something that a lot of folks may not realize about doctoral programs, and this is especially true of folks that are from out of country, working on a visa,

129
00:55:52,317 --> 00:56:07,471
is that you are very much at the mercy of the institution and especially of the advisor that you're working with. When I was in grad school I saw a number of my

130
00:56:07,471 --> 00:56:26,573
friends just have their PhD programs implode, have to be left in very precarious financial situations just because of a soured relationship with their advisor, they're expected to do unfair amounts of work, things just didn't work out.

131
00:56:27,822 --> 00:56:44,815
And when that happens, you really need to have institutions willing to back up the student in order to get any kind of fairness in resolving these in resolving these disputes.

132
00:56:44,815 --> 00:57:14,600
I mean, that's why I'm very glad to see any sort of organizing among graduate workers. I think it adds an extra level of protection to graduate students, which clearly we're seeing tonight. And I'm just really, really glad to see the work of my colleagues as well as SCIU in stepping up for the students. So thank you so much for bringing this forward. Thank you.

133
00:57:15,764 --> 00:57:42,730
Thank you. Well, since Tufts is listening, let us pass this unanimously. Let's support the unions. Let's support the workers. It's the, what Vice President Lazaro read out is unthinkable in this day and age, especially when we see, and I know there are folks in the audience here who are going to, who are planning on speaking on this later, but especially in an age where we see international students, we see immigrants,

134
00:57:43,912 --> 00:58:10,507
who formed the lifeblood of our community being ripped away. And to have a source of stability being pulled under the table, out from under the table, is, again, unthinkable. Like it or not, and this goes for everyone in this relationship, Tufts is a part of our community, like President Berry said. And that means that when someone at Tufts is

135
00:58:11,232 --> 00:58:40,156
facing such an egregious violation, we have to stand up for them. It means that in this relationship between a powerful institution and normal folks, normal workers on the ground, we got to stand for the many and not the few. This is, I mean, I'm sure folks who are here to speak can articulate this case way better than I can.

136
00:58:41,489 --> 00:59:10,008
I feel particularly connected to this in that over the last many, many weeks and months, a lot of the work I've been doing outside of the council has been focused on workers' rights. And you start to realize, I guess realize is the wrong word, I think you really fathom that in this relationship between powerful institutions and employers and

137
00:59:11,172 --> 00:59:26,174
folks who work for them, who are just trying to make a living, who are trying to live a life, there are, there exists such a disbalance of power, imbalance of power, that

138
00:59:26,343 --> 00:59:51,402
Folks can't live a normal life. They can't make, you know, money to cover their cost of living. That some of these violations are so egregious that what we hear about is only the tip of the iceberg and that many, many cases exist beyond, beneath that tip of the iceberg. And it trickles down not only to, you know, that person,

139
00:59:51,740 --> 01:00:14,774
but to the lives of those around that person as well. And again, going back to the theme of community, to the community around them. And that's why it's so important for the community to stand up and band together and say that enough is enough, that we can't let something like this happen. I'll leave it there, or else I'll keep going on.

